Q: Explain gamma risk for a short BANKNIFTY straddle on expiry day.
A: Gamma risk is the first-order derivative of the option price with respect to the underlying price. For a short straddle, gamma = ∂f/∂S = -½σ²S²/N, where σ is implied volatility, S is spot index level, and N is number of options. On expiry day, for a BANKNIFTY short straddle, gamma is negative and highly unstable. The stock price on expiry day can move sharply, triggering simultaneous option calls and puts to be exercised, causing gamma to spike dramatically. This creates a short gamma 'blow-up' risk. For example, if BANKNIFTY expires at 47,500 and moves to 49,000, both long calls and long puts will be triggered at exactly the strike price. The gamma in both directions simultaneously creates a sharp gamma blow-up on the expiry gamma surface, which is highly unstable and difficult to hedge. Practically, BANKNIFTY short straddle gamma can spike from -0.10 to -0.50 within seconds as the index moves through strike levels. Risk analysts must monitor gamma netting on expiry days, as unnetted short gamma positions (long straddles) can lose 50-100% of notional value in a tightly-structured BANKNIFTY expiry session.
============================================================

Q: What is the variance risk premium and how do you harvest it on NSE?
A: The variance risk premium (VRP) is defined as: VRP = (σF - σF*)² × E[V], where σF* is the historical standard deviation of a risk-free rate, σF is the realized standard deviation over a rolling M-month window, and E[V] is the expected value of a risk-neutral variance swap at expiry. For example, if σF* = 0.547% (historical NIFTY risk-free rate), σF = 0.304% over a 3-year horizon, and E[V] = 0.35%, then VRP ≈ (0.304% - 0.547%)² × 0.35% ≈ 0.00032% × 0.35% ≈ 0.00000112% or ~1.12 bps. On NSE, this is relevant for pricing BANKNIFTY options, short-dated NIFTY futures, and bond options. Practically, a risk analyst should: (1) calibrate σF* using MIBOR / OIS rates; (2) use historical variance estimates from NSE's own data; (3) use rolling windows (e.g., 3-year rolling std dev) to avoid data volatility. The key practical implication is that when VRP is positive, investors can charge a higher premium for variance (higher VIX expectation), making variance risk strategies potentially profitable.
============================================================

Q: How does the Carr-Madan FFT method work for option pricing?
A: The Carr-Madan FFT method allows pricing the call price as a Fourier integral: C(K,T) = (1/2π)∫∫ exp[i(ks - kL)] · S(T) · N(d1) · N(d2) · e^(-rT) dk ds, where k = ln(S) and L = ln(K). Converting to a finite sum using the FFT, the key dimension reduction arises from the relationship: d1 = σ√(T) · (ln(S/K) + (r+σ²/2)T) and d2 = d1 - σ√(T). The FFT computation on N(d1) and N(d2) reduces the computational complexity from O(N²) to O(N log N), making pricing thousands of strikes and expiries on NIFTY options (typically 25-50 strikes per expiry) feasible with standard CUDA GPUs. Practically, this is critical for BANKNIFTY weekly options, where the Black-Scholes PDE approach would be computationally infeasible. The method also naturally handles stochastic volatility surfaces by updating σ dynamically, making it suitable for BANKNIFTY options pricing under GARCH models.
